WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.169
Some scenes don't fit in a single frame.

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:07.730
LumiRaw's merge page combines several shots of the same scene into one.

00:00:08.034 --> 00:00:09.913
There are five ways to do it.

00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:11.792
Exposure merge, for brackets.

00:00:11.792 --> 00:00:15.874
Average, for less noise, or a long exposure made of short ones.

00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:18.530
Median, to remove people walking through.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.057
Lighten, for star trails and fireworks.

00:00:21.057 --> 00:00:25.204
And focus stack, for pictures that are sharp from front to back.

00:00:25.604 --> 00:00:27.484
Here's a bracket of the coast:

00:00:27.484 --> 00:00:32.058
one frame for the sky, and one over three stops brighter for the shadows.

00:00:32.058 --> 00:00:36.694
LumiRaw reads each frame's exposure, and tells you if something looks off.

00:00:37.098 --> 00:00:37.852
Shot by hand?

00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:42.088
It lines the frames up for you, and removes ghosts where something moved.

00:00:42.852 --> 00:00:46.979
And the merged picture opens in the developer like any other raw file,

00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:50.812
with both the bright sky and the deep shadows there to work with.
